# punused findings adjudicated as false positives. One literal substring per
# line, matched against punused's output line; full-line `#` comments carry the
# reason. Invalid-by-construction classes: `.kiro/steering/tools.md`.
#
# toolbelt is a LIBRARY (`github.com/cplieger/toolbelt/v2`) that also ships a
# `main` package under cmd/toolcatalog, so the CI gate's app-mode detection
# ("a main package exists") gates it on punused even though its exported
# surface is a PUBLISHED API. punused resolves references through gopls
# `textDocument/references` inside this module only -- its README's first
# caveat, "It does not detect references from outside of your project" -- so
# every API symbol whose production callers live in a consumer module is
# reported EU1002 (no reference) or EU1001 (referenced only from this repo's
# _test.go files). Consumer evidence below was taken against the two modules
# whose go.mod requires this one (vibekit v2.2.12, web-terminal-kiro v2.2.8);
# consumer line numbers are the snapshot at adjudication time, the file and
# call shape are the durable part. Cross-checked: `deadcode -test ./...` and
# `staticcheck -checks U1000 ./...` both report nothing in this module, so no
# finding below is dead code hiding behind a published name.
#
# Engine lifecycle, the four calls a consumer app makes to own a tools engine.
# New is the only constructor (toolbelt.go:192): vibekit
# internal/composition/composition.go:477, web-terminal-kiro main.go:1056.
# Close is the shutdown half (toolbelt.go:231): vibekit composition.go:288
# (`a.tools.Close()`, field typed *toolbelt.Engine at composition.go:45),
# web-terminal-kiro main.go:1007. Wait blocks on a job id (engine.go:158):
# web-terminal-kiro main.go:1131 inside awaitBootConvergence, launched at
# main.go:1108. Reconcile enqueues the convergence job (engine.go:583):
# vibekit composition.go:505, web-terminal-kiro main.go:1089.
function New
method (*Engine).Close
method (*Engine).Wait
method (*Engine).Reconcile
#
# EnsureInstalled (engine.go:609) is the synchronous "a product action needs
# this binary now" path. vibekit assigns it as a method VALUE rather than
# calling it -- `forgesPkg.EnsureTool = toolsEngine.EnsureInstalled`,
# internal/composition/composition.go:156 -- so even an in-module reference
# search for a call expression would miss it; the forge login path invokes it
# through that function field (internal/forges/install.go).
method (*Engine).EnsureInstalled
#
# DefaultSeed (toolbelt.go:249) is the shared starter manifest every consumer
# passes as Config.Seed: vibekit composition.go:483, web-terminal-kiro
# main.go:1061. Deleting it would leave both apps with no manifest seed and
# duplicate the language-server starter set into each of them.
function DefaultSeed
#
# ReconcileMissing (wire.go:129) is doubly invalid: it is the mode
# web-terminal-kiro's boot reconcile passes (main.go:1089), AND it is the
# iota-0 sentinel of ReconcileMode (wire.go:126-133), so deleting it would
# renumber ReconcileFull -- a wire-level behavior change for any consumer that
# has the old value compiled in, not a cleanup.
constant ReconcileMissing
#
# The three consumer-contract helpers, each with two call sites and no
# in-module production caller by design (they exist FOR the consumer):
# ParseRequireList (catalog.go:222) -- vibekit main.go:67, web-terminal-kiro
# main.go:1049, both feeding CatalogRefresh.Require from an embedded
# required-tools.txt; DefaultCatalogURL (catalogrefresh.go:51) -- the env
# default at vibekit internal/composition/config.go:96 and web-terminal-kiro
# main.go:340, the one place a publisher move stays a one-line change;
# ParseCatalogRefresh (catalogrefresh.go:71) -- vibekit config.go:97,
# web-terminal-kiro main.go:203, which owns the clamp policy both apps apply
# to their refresh env var.
function ParseRequireList
constant DefaultCatalogURL
function ParseCatalogRefresh
#
# httpapi.Handler (httpapi/httpapi.go:82) is the entire purpose of the
# httpapi package: the REST projection a consumer mounts on its own mux.
# vibekit internal/server/server.go:224 (`httpapi.Handler(s.tools,
# "/api/tools")`, field typed *toolbelt.Engine at server.go:43),
# web-terminal-kiro routes.go:177 behind loopbackOnly. Nothing in this module
# mounts a mux, so an in-module reference can only ever come from a test.
function Handler
#
# flag.Value contract, not an API symbol: multiFlag is an UNEXPORTED type in
# package main (cmd/toolcatalog/main.go:69) whose two exported methods exist
# solely to satisfy stdlib `flag.Value` at the `fl.Var(&overlays, "overlay",
# ...)` registration on main.go:82. punused strips the receiver and tests only
# the method name (run.go's handleSymbol), so it reports them as unused
# exported symbols although no other package can reach them; the use is a
# compile-time interface satisfaction, which gopls references does not model.
# Deleting either one breaks the build, not just the -overlay flag.
method (*multiFlag).String
method (*multiFlag).Set
#
# errors.Is contract: (*DependentsError).Is (engine.go:43) is the method
# `errors.Is` looks for through its `interface{ Is(error) bool }` type
# assertion, which is a runtime dispatch gopls cannot resolve to a reference.
# It is what makes `errors.Is(err, ErrHasDependents)` match the *DependentsError
# returned by engine.go:360 and engine.go:545, and it is documented as the
# published comparison contract at engine.go:13. Test-covered at
# engine_test.go:255, engine_test.go:945 and reconcile_test.go:112, so deleting
# it fails the suite rather than silently, but it is still not dead: it is the
# only reason the sentinel comparison this package documents works at all.
method (*DependentsError).Is
